For the meantime, let's get started, shall we?

45
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:28,000
I should start out by saying there's really no set definition of what a high-adventure trip is.

46
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:37,000
I mean, there's no minimum number of days or minimum number of crew members or minimum number of miles that you have to cover or anything like that.

47
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:43,000
What this is, it's a trip that is aimed at more senior scouts.

48
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Most of the high-adventure bases now require scouts to be at least 13..

49
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:57,000
And it is a multi-day experience, maybe backpacking or canoeing or on a sailboat or something like that.

50
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
So don't get tied up in. there's a specific definition of a high-adventure trip,

51
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
but I'm just going to use that term to identify what we're talking about.

52
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,000
I might talk about crews and I might talk about troops here,

53
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:18,000
And yes, there are some official definitions of what a Boy Scout troop and a venture crew is and what venturers are.

54
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,000
I know that within our way of managing high-adventure trips,

55
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:29,000
when we get the people who are interested in the trip signed on, we form crews.

56
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,000
So I'm going to be using the word crew here within the context of what we do as a scout troop,

57
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:42,000
But don't confuse it with the fact that you have to have a venture crew to be able to participate in high-adventure

58
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
or to have a high-adventure crew.

59
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
That's not necessarily so. It's something that you can create within a troop.

60
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Now, that doesn't mean that you're going to be able to get the insignia and the uniforming of a venture crew,

61
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,000
but I think you're understanding what I'm saying

62
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:06,000
And I don't want to confuse you with these terms. as we talk about the subject,

63
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Thousands of scouts participate and enjoy the big three high-adventure bases each year.

64
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:16,000
I'm talking about Fillmont in New Mexico, where there's backpacking,

65
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:21,000
our sea base in Florida, where you're on a sailboat,

66
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:27,000
and Northern Tier in Eli, Minnesota, where it's canoeing.

67
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:32,000
These are the three most familiar high-adventure bases

68
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,000
and there are literally dozens of other council-based programs.

69
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,000
They might be backpacking or canoeing or something like that,

70
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
but there's a good many of them out there.

71
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
I have colleagues in scouting who recommend these things highly,

72
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:52,000
and I want to talk about more of the other end of the spectrum in this series

73
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
and that are troop and crew-based programs that explore a little bit less known territory.

74
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:05,000
I will tell you this: I have led high-adventure type experiences inside and outside of scouting.

75
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
I've led and participated in a good many of them.

76
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:13,000
They might be backpacking, they might be climbing, they might be canoeing or something like that,

77
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:17,000
but they all have one thing in common, and this is it:

78
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:26,000
The people who go, and their level of preparation is always more important than the place that you go.

79
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:31,000
So if you'll just lay aside the idea that you have to spend thousands of dollars to travel

80
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
and thousands of dollars on equipment and things like that,

81
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
and just think about the fact that you're going to take a group

82
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
and you're going to have a certain set of goals and challenges

83
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,000
and you're going to go on this multi-day high-adventure thing,

84
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
that is what I really want you to center on,

85
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:52,000
because preparing your group to go, your crew to go,

86
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:56,000
is the most important part of the entire thing.

87
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:02,000
Here's an example: You're going to go to Fillmont, the crown jewel of scout camps,

88
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
and the skies are going to stay blue the whole time you're there.

89
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,000
It's not going to rain and the temperatures are going to be just right.

90
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:10,000
It's going to be nice and warm during the day.

91
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
It's going to be nice and cool at night. There's not going to be any bugs,

92
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:18,000
You're going to have five-star meals and it's just. you know,

93
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,000
it looks like it's going to be a wonderful trip.

94
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:24,000
Well, if you're not prepared properly and you go there with a dysfunctional crew,

95
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:29,000
it's going to be like the Batan Death March, I guarantee you.

96
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:34,000
Likewise, if you spend a few days hiking in a state park, somewhere

97
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:38,000
you know maybe less inspiring, in an environment,

98
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:46,000
and it rains every single day, and you're eating oatmeal and ramen,

99
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:49,000
If you have a sharp crew who knows their stuff

100
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:55,000
and who's able to get along together, it's a lifelong golden memory.

101
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:00,000
Yeah, it is that important.

102
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:05,000
High Adventure should take a crew to a new level of experience in the outdoors

103
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:07,000
and in developing those things that are the aim of scouting,

104
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:12,000
the skills and the competencies that we want to develop as scouting,

105
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:17,000
Growing confidence in their abilities and their ability to lead

106
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
and their opportunity to follow

107
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:22,000
and their appreciation for the abilities of others

108
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:25,000
are all part of a successful High Adventure trip.

109
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:28,000
These trips don't have to be ambitious,

110
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:30,000
They don't have to be expensive,

111
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:32,000
They don't have to be far-flung-

112
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:34,000
I mean they can be on the other side of the world

113
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:37,000
or the other side of the state or an hour from your house.

114
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:41,000
What really matters is the crew and the way it prepared for the trip.

115
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
You know good weather and good food and good surroundings

116
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:47,000
are part of the picture, but the biggest part of the challenge

117
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:51,000
and the satisfaction of accomplishing the goals

118
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:57,000
is setting some good goals and knowing their importance to the trip

119
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:01,000
and building on them by thoroughly preparing the crew to accomplish them.

120
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:05,000
Now here's a couple of things that figure into your rationale

121
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:10,000
of building a High Adventure program that's based in your troop or your crew.

122
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:14,000
It's based on a few logistic and philosophic criteria.

123
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:19,000
One of the most important considerations is time and money.

124
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Yeah, I know it's not that romantic,

125
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:26,000
but it is going to come down to time and money.

126
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:30,000
A lot of High Adventure trips involve- for us

127
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:35,000
where we live in the mid-Atlantic

128
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:41,000
involve air travel and ground fees when we get there,

129
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:46,000
and the cost for participating in one of those is going to be

130
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
well over $1,000 for us.

131
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:54,000
It also requires 12 or 14 days, including travel time.

132
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:58,000
Now, in comparison, the trips that we've put together as a troop

133
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:03,000
cost about half as much, usually about $500 to $600.

134
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:07,000
And they run from early Friday to the following Saturday night,

135
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
and they only consume six vacation days.

136
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Now, this is a really important consideration,

137
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:18,000
because you're going to need adult leadership to participate in these trips

138
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:22,000
and a lot of people aren't going to be able to take the two-week vacation

139
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:27,000
that is demanded of some of these more ambitious trips.

140
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:33,000
The other thing is, here's an interesting way of thinking about your High Adventure plans.

141
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:38,000
You want to ride a roller coaster or you want to pedal a bike?

142
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
What do I mean by that?

143
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:46,000
Well, a lot of the national programs are pre-planned, pre-set programs

144
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:49,000
and they involve a minimal amount of flexibility.

145
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
The dates and the activities, the equipment and the routes

146
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
are pretty well predetermined.

147
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:58,000
There's also a good deal of competition for the best routes and times,

148
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:02,000
and that I liken to lining up for a roller coaster ride.

149
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:06,000
I mean you buy your ticket and you get in line.

150
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Troop-based or crew-based High Adventure trips are a lot more flexible

151
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:14,000
and they're more like getting on your bike and heading out for a ride.

152
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
Not that the roller coaster isn't fun and challenging.

153
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
it's just a different approach.

154
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
I'm not saying that one is better than the other,

155
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
but this is the rationale that we went through

156
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:27,000
when we were putting our High Adventure troop program together.

157
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:31,000
What does it take to put a troop High Adventure program together?

158
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Well, it's not as difficult as you might think.

159
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:35,000
Where do you go and what do you do?

160
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,000
Well, the simple answer to that question is: just about anywhere

161
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
and just about anything.

162
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
What are your scouts interested in doing?

163
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
Are they interested in a week of backpacking?

164
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
Maybe canoeing?

165
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Maybe just touring?

166
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:51,000
There's nothing to say that you don't rent a van

167
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:54,000
and you go visit several historic sites or something like that,

168
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:58,000
during the time that you set aside for a High Adventure trip.

169
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
Maybe they're interested in cycling,

170
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
or maybe they're interested in boating.

171
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
Now you choose.

172
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:09,000
Making a trip happen requires four things.

173
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:12,000
The first is vision and leadership.

174
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
That's easy to say,

175
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
but it's more important than you might think.

176
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
Somebody has to say

177
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
let's go,

178
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
And then they have to show the other folks

179
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:26,000
in the troop that it's actually possible.

180
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
This can be a little more challenging than you think,

181
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,000
especially with your first troop-based trip,

182
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
But I'll tell you something

183
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
From experience: it's possible.

184
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
It's not only possible,

185
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
It's not as hard as you might think.

186
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
It's not only not as hard as you might think.

187
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:45,000
it gets to be easy the more trips that you do.

188
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:49,000
The second thing out of four that it takes is details.

189
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:52,000
Troop-based trips are full of details

190
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:55,000
and preparation time is going to take

191
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
at least as many hours as the trip itself

192
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,000
and probably a little bit more.

193
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
But once you can sign yourself to the fact

194
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:05,000
that you're going to be doing a lot of detail work,

195
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:09,000
then it's not quite so onerous.

196
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,000
Third is a commitment to training and skill development.

197
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:16,000
Now, whoever's going to lead or advise participants

198
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
has to commit time to training

199
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:22,000
and learning the applicable skills

200
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,000
to safely carry out the plan.

201
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,000
And fourth is going to be finances.

202
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
Now, calculating the cost of the trip

203
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
and collecting money and paying it out

204
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
and accounting for all of that

205
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
is an important aspect.

206
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,000
There might be fundraising with it.

207
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
It's all very important stuff.

208
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
And those are the four things

209
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
that we're going to talk about.

210
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:47,000
The first: vision and leadership.

211
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
It's the most important step,

212
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
because without any vision you're not going anywhere.

213
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:54,000
It's important to establish and protect the vision

214
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
and to build anticipation and excitement with it,

215
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
because the details and the questions

216
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
are going to try and kill the vision.

217
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
If we want to motivate people,

218
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
we need to get a picture of exactly what we're going to do.

219
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:09,000
And one of our first canoe trips to Canada

220
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
we sat around our campfire on the first night

221
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:14,000
after getting to a very hard one campsite

222
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,000
And it was dark and it was difficult

223
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
to get ourselves there.

224
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
A day of paddling and portaging

225
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
and a frustrating search for the campsite-

226
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
and the crew got separated-

227
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
had me pretty rung out

228
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
and I was lamenting over a couple of mistakes

229
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
that I made to one of my fellow adult leaders

230
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
who was sitting there at the fire

231
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
making our dinner

232
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:38,000
and he looked at me kind of incredulously

233
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
and he said: hey, look,

234
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
here we are, we're camped out on a lake in Canada

235
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
and we're going to have a steak dinner.

236
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
What more do you want?

237
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
So he had maintained the vision

238
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
while I was bogged down in details.

239
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:53,000
Getting that first troop high adventure trip

240
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
off the ground required helping my scouts

241
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
envision it as a real possibility.

242
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
I was surprised at how skeptical they were.

243
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,000
I guess it all seemed a little too good to be true

244
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
or too logistically challenging to launch.

245
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:08,000
The adults in the troop also required

246
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
a bit of convincing.

247
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
You know, some questioned if we were allowed

248
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
to do such things.

249
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
some were concerned about the cost,

250
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
some were concerned about safety

251
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
and some were concerned about all three

252
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
or four things like that.

253
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
But they did finally get a hold of the idea

254
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:27,000
and we were able to bring it into reality.

255
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
So establishing the vision

256
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
and then fanning the flames

257
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
and motivating scouts to maintain their resolve

258
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
through this forest of details

259
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:38,000
is as important as the details themselves.

260
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:41,000
Niels van der Rohe once said, famously,

261
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
God is in the details,

262
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
So let's talk about the detail part of it.

263
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
So the place that you're going to go,

264
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
there might be a state or a provincial

265
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:50,000
or a national park,

266
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
and these are very likely destinations

267
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
and each is going to have its own

268
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
particular rules and procedures.

269
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
and, of course, getting online

270
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
and getting an idea of exactly

271
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
what you're in for is a good start.

272
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
A lot of times, popular places

273
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
will have a lot of online trip reports

274
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
and reviews, and those can be very informative.

275
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
I'd suggest that once you think

276
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
you have all the information

277
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
that you can glean off the web.

278
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
you pick up the phone and call someone

279
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
who's interested in talking to you-

280
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
Sometimes it'll be a park ranger

281
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
or somebody who runs a concession

282
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
in one of the parks-

283
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
and confirm the information that you have is correct.

284
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
Outfitters or other local businesses

285
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
are usually more than happy

286
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:33,000
to talk to you about putting a trip together.

287
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
I would just get out and do

288
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
and let the miles take care of themselves.

289
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:41,000
but when you do start thinking about mileage,

290
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,000
you need to have the context of terrain.

291
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
a mile that gains 2,000 feet in elevation

292
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
is a lot different from a flat mile.

293
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
The trail conditions, water,

294
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
weather and camping regulations-

295
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
they'll affect the time and effort

296
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
that are required to cover a distance.

297
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
so if you're planning a hike

298
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
as a part of your high adventure,

299
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
you need to become very expert in these things.

300
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
and you need to become expert

301
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
in planning trail distances

302
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
and route selection,

303
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
and it's probably one of the more crucial choices

304
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
in making or breaking your trip.

305
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
you need to gauge this route selection

306
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
with the cruise ability

307
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
and your familiarity with the terrain.

308
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
The next detail is people.

309
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
if you want-

310
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
this isn't a rule, it's kind of a rule of thumb-

311
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
cruise should probably be more than 5,

312
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
less than 12.

313
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
now some destinations will regulate

314
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
the maximum group size.

315
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
ratios of adults to youth are important.

316
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
when we go canoeing

317
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
we usually have crews of 7 or 9,

318
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
with the maximum and minimum

319
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
of 3 adults over the age of 21,

320
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
because if we have

321
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
an equal number of adults and scouts

322
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
or we have more adults than scouts,

323
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
then the trip becomes diminished.

324
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
as far as the scout experience goes,

325
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
the scout should always outnumber

326
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
the adults, in my opinion.

327
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
I'm careful to explain that the adventure

328
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
is 4: scouts

329
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
and adults are along for the ride

330
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
and we make sure

331
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
that our adults understand that,

332
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
and sometimes we have to turn them away

333
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
because we already have the maximum

334
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
number that we want to have.

335
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
so deciding who goes

336
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
and how many, that's another important detail.

337
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
getting there and back-

338
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
the transportation part of the trip- is another

339
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
set of details: rentals

340
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
and fares and timetables and drivers

341
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
and group rates and insurance

342
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
and accommodations are all going to demand your attention.

343
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
the gear that's going to be required-

344
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
can it be rented

345
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
or does it have to be purchased by your troop

346
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
or does it get purchased by individuals?

347
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
there's probably going to be a mix of all 3.

348
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
the quality and utility

349
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
of your gear is important

350
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
and it will require a little bit of research.

351
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
what looks inexpensive and indulgent

352
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
at home may prove to be cheap

353
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
and indispensable when you're

354
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
8 hours away

355
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
from the nearest store,

356
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
so learning about

357
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
the gear that's required and making sure

358
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
that you get the best stuff that you can afford

359
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
is very important.

360
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
food is another one of the details

361
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
that you're going to come up with.

362
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
the appropriate weight, quantity and nutritional value

363
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
of a backcountry diet

364
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
is a pretty big task,

365
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
especially when you expand it into 5 or 10 people,

366
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
and then buying it and packing it

367
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
after it's planned

368
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
is also a pretty big task.

369
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
in my experience, it's best to have

370
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
at least 2 or 3 crew members assigned

371
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
to doing different parts

372
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
of that task and making sure that it happens.

373
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
training and skill development

374
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
is important,

375
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
and one of the skills

376
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
that you're going to have is

377
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
planning a menu and purchasing it

378
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
for this kind of a trip.

379
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
fortunately, the BSA offers

380
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
a number of excellent online courses

381
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
that will prepare you

382
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
to conduct a safe high

383
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
adventure trip.

384
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
weather hazards training

385
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
track, safely climb on, safely

386
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
safe swim, defense

387
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
and safety afloat are all excellent

388
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
online courses that I recommend highly to you,

389
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
and I would take

390
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
all of the ones that are available

391
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
online,

392
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
because they all have common elements

393
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
and it will help build a

394
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
consciousness of safety

395
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
and of risk management

396
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
that will assist you

397
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
in making sure that your scouts

398
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
come back from their high adventure trip

399
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
a little worn out

400
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
and maybe a little tired,

401
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
but ready to go back and challenge things again,

402
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
and that you have a good,

403
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
safe experience when you're out in the wilderness.

404
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
high adventure

405
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
means, to a certain extent,

406
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
higher risk than

407
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
we are undertaking when we are in.

408
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
you know our general

409
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
troop campouts,

410
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
because we're going to put ourselves

411
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
in a wilderness situation, there's a likelihood

412
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
that we're going to be several hours

413
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
away from any kind of emergency

414
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
response system. within the past

415
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
several years,

416
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
the BSA has begun requiring

417
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
that people who go to

418
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
Filmont and people who go to

419
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
the other national high adventure bases

420
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
have someone in the crew

421
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
who is certified in wilderness

422
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
first responder-

423
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
the wilderness first responder training.

424
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
when I took it a year ago

425
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
it was a 16 hour course done over the course of

426
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
two days. it emphasized

427
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
how we could assess

428
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
injuries and illnesses

429
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
and how we could keep people stable

430
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
until the professional responders

431
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
were able to get to us.

432
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
it is absolutely

433
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
invaluable and it should be

434
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
a required component

435
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
of your training

436
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
if you're going to conduct a high adventure trip.

437
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
there are a lot of unfortunate

438
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
stories out there of people who go into the

439
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
wilderness woefully unprepared,

440
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
unfamiliar with their surroundings,

441
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
unfamiliar with the situation

442
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
that they're going to encounter,

443
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
and they end up in

444
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
injury or being lost for several

445
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
days or some kind of

446
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
just terrible, awful

447
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
story that

448
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
you do not want to repeat.

449
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
as the leader of a group of scouts

450
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
on a high adventure trip,

451
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
this shouldn't scare you away from it,

452
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
but it should give you an appreciation

453
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
of how important it is

454
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
to have a good

455
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
risk management mindset

456
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
and to have the skills

457
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
to manage problems

458
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
when they arise.

459
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
if you're old enough to participate

460
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
in a high adventure trip,

461
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
most scouts should have some pretty solid

462
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
general camping skills.

463
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
they should be familiar with how to put up

464
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
their tents and how to cook

465
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
and things like that.

466
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
but I would make sure that

467
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
any preparation for a high adventure trip

468
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
emphasizes those particular skills

469
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
that will be needed

470
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
once they are out there in the wilderness

471
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
and they're on their high adventure track.

472
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
by experience I've learned

473
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
at night in the woods in the wilderness-

474
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
you'll want to train them to do that

475
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
at your troop meeting place

476
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
without a whole lot of stress

477
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
and difficulty.

478
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
so making sure that

479
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
everybody in the crew has a good grasp

480
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
of the skills that are going to be particular

481
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
to the trip, whether it's hanging bear bags

482
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
or purifying water or cooking

483
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
is going to be an important aspect

484
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
of preparation for the trip.

485
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
so a high adventure program

486
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
need not be expensive,

487
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
it need not be elaborate,

488
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
it need not be difficult.

489
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
it is something that any troop

490
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
can participate in.

491
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
whether you are headed off to one of the national

492
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
high adventure bases,

493
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
whether you're doing a council based trip

494
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
or, as we have been discussing,

495
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
building your own kind of high adventure trip,

496
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
it is definitely

497
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
worth the effort.

498
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
it need not be expensive,

499
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
it need not be elaborate,

500
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
but it will be something that your

501
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
will remember for years to come.

502
